ABSTRACT: There is a growing need for strong security measures to combat cyberattacks with increasing data consumption and reliance on interconnected networks. This is especially required at the network edge, where the data are present and first acquired. Cyber attacks such as distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attacks and others pose risk and various other inconveniences to all stakeholder involved. The dynamic nature of these attacks and the lack of processing power at the edge require new methods to tackle cyber-attacks. This paper proposes a novel approach for network anomaly detection and mitigation that uses edge AI and the contextual understanding power of Large Language Models (LLMs). The system utilizes deep learning methods to identify cyberattacks where the models are deployed on edge devices and servers enabling quick identification of anomalies. The paper then explores the use of LLMs to automate the resolution and mitigation of these attacks in real-time, leading to lower impact by reducing downtimes and increasing availability. This research aims to contribute to the field of network security by proposing an edge AI driven solution for real-time local DDoS attack detection and the utilization of LLMs for network anomaly resolution and system adaptation. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0143] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Access | type=Article
TITLE: Transformation of Vulnerability Management Through Artificial Intelligence: An Overview of Generative and Learning Models
KEYWORDS: AI; AppSec; Cybersecurity; DevSecOps; LLM; security scanning; vulnerability analysis; vulnerability management; Abstracting; Codes (symbols); Computer programming languages; Cybersecurity; Data flow analysis; Data privacy; Graphic methods; Learning systems; Network security; Pattern matching; Risk management; Semantics; Software engineering; Ai; Appsec; Cyber security; Devsecop; Generative model; Learning models; Llm; Security scanning; Vulnerability analysis; Vulnerability management; Failure analysis
ABSTRACT: Software teams now uncover far more vulnerabilities than they can reasonably triage or fix. At the same time, learning-based analysis and large code models have matured. They can now move beyond pattern matching and into semantic reasoning over code, binaries, dependencies, and infrastructure-as-code (IaC). This article brings these threads together. We organize the field around how models ‘‘see’’ software (tokens and sequences; Abstract Syntax Trees (ASTs)/Control Flow Graphs (CFGs)/Data Flow Graphs (DFGs)/Code Property Graphs (CPGs); Intermediate Representations (IRs) and bytecode; and deployment/configuration artifacts). We then compare families of detectors on both technical and operational grounds. Building on that view, we outline a risk-aware prioritization model. This model combines signals of likelihood, exposure, and impact into clear, auditable policies that reduce effort for the same level of exploited coverage. We also examine how large models can assist with scoring and remediation. Finally, we specify the guardrails-tests, static checks, and policy constraints-needed to keep generated changes reliable and traceable. The result is a practical map: where to integrate capabilities in Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD), what evidence to retain for audit, and how to measure success using program metrics such as triage time, developer acceptance, and change-failure rate. The paper closes with concrete research directions in multi-modal learning, privacy-preserving training, and safe autonomy for remediation. © 2026 The Authors.

