ABSTRACT: The pace of the implementation of AI systems in a manufacturing setup has surpassed the engineering standards required to ensure that they are dependable, protected and can be scaled. Even though, the sphere of multi-agent architecture, machine learning infrastructure, operational monitoring, etc. have been largely improved, they are still seen as isolated results scattered across the literature, which results in the absence of a single pattern of constructing and operating trusted AI systems within production settings. The framework consists of three layers that are interrelated. The Architecture Layer offers a design model of Multi-agent systems, namely Agent Role Definition, Coordination Strategy and State Management and enables the Complex Workflow to be broken down into manageable parts. It is based on the data base and offers an opportunity to construct a scalable machine learning pipeline and direct the transition of a traditional model to a large language model. The operational layer consists of preventive security controls (e.g., scoping, rate-limiting, and circuit-breaker) and automatic detection mechanisms, with artificial intelligence used to check artificial intelligence systems, such as graph-based anomaly detection and risk assessment. The framework emphasizes that it cannot be entirely dependent on any of the levels but is premised on uniform engineering practices throughout the entire system life cycle. Another way the framework can be adapted to various classes of engineering resources, described in this paper, is between start-ups and large tech companies. The aim of this study is to give a single reference model to help the engineering team to overcome the problem of AI transitioning prototype to production without compromising the safety and reliability required in real world applications. © 2026 IEEE.

